# =============================================================================
# fleet-ops#4266 — who stopped it? audit trail
# =============================================================================
# A clean `systemctl stop` of a healthy unit yields Result=success, so
# OnFailure never fires and the escort rail stays dark. The only durable
# trace of "who stopped it" is the execve audit record of the stopping
# process. Watch both mechanisms a stopper can use under this uid:
#   - /usr/bin/systemctl execve (systemctl --user stop <unit>)
#   - /usr/bin/kill      execve (kill <pid> of the unit's main process)
# plus their /bin symlink aliases (Ubuntu ships /usr/bin paths; Debian
# merges into /usr/bin too; the /bin/ symlink exists on both).
#
# Bound to auid=1000 (the fleet user) only: root/system maintenance stops
# are visible in the same records anyway via exe, and -F auid=1000 keeps
# the noise down to the fleet user's own stopping commands.
#
# Install: sudo -n install -D -m 0644 -o root -g root this file to /etc/audit/rules.d/ and
# reloads via `sudo augenrules --load` (idempotent; auditd must be
# installed).
#
# Query (fleet-ops#5918): run as root —
#   /usr/sbin/ausearch -k fleet-unit-stop -ts yesterday -i
# The absolute path is mandatory: ExecStopPost and the systemd user manager
# run with PATH=/home/nish/.local/bin:/usr/local/bin:/usr/bin:/bin, which has
# no /usr/sbin — and ausearch ships at /usr/sbin/ausearch. A bare `ausearch`
# (or the deleted bin/fleet-who-stopped wrapper, removed 2026-09-18 with the
# glue sweep) finds nothing there and reports `no audit trail` on a host
# where the trail exists. See #4733 (same fix for the canary) and #5456
# addendum J (no silent path left in prose).
# =============================================================================
-w /usr/bin/systemctl -p x -F auid=1000 -k fleet-unit-stop
-w /usr/bin/kill      -p x -F auid=1000 -k fleet-unit-stop
-w /bin/systemctl     -p x -F auid=1000 -k fleet-unit-stop
-w /bin/kill          -p x -F auid=1000 -k fleet-unit-stop
# =============================================================================
# fleet-ops#4266 — raw systemd-run forensic trail
# =============================================================================
# The escalation canary's detached-work lint (block 13) that read this trail
# was deleted 2026-09-18 with the glue sweep; the six -w rules stay as the
# on-disk forensic evidence for the #4266 silent-death vector (a bare
# `systemd-run --user` transient — no OnFailure, no RuntimeMaxSec, no
# ExecStopPost deliverable check — is only reconstructable from these execve
# records). The compliant shape is the README one-liner: a transient unit
# carrying both -p RuntimeMaxSec= and the -p ExecStopPost= deliverable check.
# Watch the systemd-run binary (and its /bin symlink) so the trail stays
# populated. Key fleet-unit-run is distinct from the fleet-unit-stop key
# above (who-stopped) so the two queries stay clean.
#
# Query (fleet-ops#5918): run as root with the absolute path —
#   /usr/sbin/ausearch -k fleet-unit-run -ts yesterday -i
# (bare `ausearch` fails on the systemd-user PATH; see the fleet-unit-stop
# block above for why.)
# =============================================================================
-w /usr/bin/systemd-run -p x -F auid=1000 -k fleet-unit-run
-w /bin/systemd-run     -p x -F auid=1000 -k fleet-unit-run
